findings.description:
Lines 45 and 87 require the strategy to be swappable between brute force, spatial hash, SciPy KD-tree and GPU "without touching the force computation," but they never say what the interface returns. A KD-tree returns ragged per-agent lists. Vectorized NumPy force code wants flat pair arrays (CSR/COO with `i`, `j`, `dx` and `dist`). A GPU path wants fixed-k or cell-binned layouts. These are different contracts, and the force code in each case is shaped by the result format. Lines 87 and 99 together demand "no per-agent Python branching" over variable-length neighborhoods, so the representation must be chosen now. The document also doesn't say:
- how the index is built per tick, since an index over the full population costs O(N) even when the active subset is small, which erodes the multi-resolution savings;
- how heterogeneous neighbors (different profiles, line 91) are filtered;
- what the perception radius is, per profile or per force.

I'd pick a flat pair-list output (`i_idx`, `j_idx`, `delta`, `dist²`) as the contract and treat other formats as adapters.

Line 47 gives the environment as a read-only interface but defines no shape. A per-agent scalar `elevation(x, y)` call would violate the "no callbacks into Python during the hot loop" rule (line 99). The interface needs to be a bulk query (`elevation(xy_array) -> array`) or pre-sampled grids passed in as tick input. The engine also has no stated behavior for:
- world boundaries (wrap, reflect or steer-away force, which 300-arch line 84 says affect spatial indexing);
- max speed and max force clamps, which are the normal fix for the integration instability raised in line 93 and are not mentioned;
- coincident agents (zero-distance separation giving division by zero or NaN);
- invalid `profile_index` values;
- NaN propagation across the shared array, where one bad agent poisons its neighbors through neighbor forces.

Error handling is assumed throughout and never addressed.
